WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:04.280
Okay.

00:04.280 --> 00:08.160
So now we're going to focus on how to use the subquery in the Where clause.

00:08.200 --> 00:14.400
So now in SQL as we learned we can go and filter the tables using the Where clause by using like static

00:14.400 --> 00:15.040
values.

00:15.040 --> 00:20.400
But now in real data projects we're going to go and filter the data based on like complex logic.

00:20.520 --> 00:27.480
So now in order to prepare this complex logic, we go and use the subqueries in order to make like dynamic

00:27.480 --> 00:29.760
filtering for our main tables.

00:29.800 --> 00:34.520
And now in order to filter data using the Where clause, we have to go and use operators and we can

00:34.520 --> 00:36.040
split it into two groups.

00:36.040 --> 00:38.920
We have the comparison operators and another set.

00:38.920 --> 00:43.600
We can call it logical operators or sometimes we call it subqueries operators.

00:43.640 --> 00:46.480
So now first we're going to talk about the comparison operators.

00:46.480 --> 00:52.040
So the are operators that we can use in order to compare two values in order to help us filtering the

00:52.040 --> 00:54.240
data based on specific condition.

00:54.280 --> 00:59.160
And now in SQL basics we have learned that we have different comparison operators and they are very

00:59.160 --> 00:59.480
simple.

00:59.520 --> 01:05.320
So in order to compare two values we have operator like the equal we have as well not equal the opposite.

01:05.320 --> 01:11.070
So we have greater than less than and as well we have greater than or equal to.

01:11.310 --> 01:14.190
And the last one we have less than or equal to.

01:14.230 --> 01:15.390
So they are very simple.

01:15.390 --> 01:21.190
Now instead of comparing two values, we're going to go and compare a value with the result of a subquery

01:21.230 --> 01:23.030
using the comparison operators.

01:23.070 --> 01:23.390
All right.

01:23.390 --> 01:28.830
Let's check the syntax of the subquery inside the Where clause using the comparison operators.

01:28.830 --> 01:33.670
So we start with the standard stuff where we say select few columns that you want to retrieve.

01:33.710 --> 01:37.710
And we want to get the data directly from specific table in our database.

01:37.750 --> 01:41.230
And now we come to the where condition where we want to filter the table.

01:41.230 --> 01:42.350
So we say where.

01:42.390 --> 01:45.790
And then we select specific column from the table one.

01:45.790 --> 01:50.430
Now since we are talking about the comparison operators, we can go with the operator for example equal.

01:50.470 --> 01:55.390
And usually we go and specify here like static value like a number or string.

01:55.390 --> 02:01.670
But instead of having a static value what we can do, we can get the value from another Select statement,

02:01.670 --> 02:07.150
another query like here we're saying select a column from table two and with the filter.

02:07.150 --> 02:13.310
So now whatever comes from this subquery is going to be used in order to filter the table number one.

02:13.310 --> 02:18.820
And of course we are telling SQL this is a subquery by defining the parenthesis at the start and at

02:18.820 --> 02:21.620
the ends, and the outer query is going to be the main query.

02:21.620 --> 02:26.740
So as you can see, we are using the subquery in order to filter the main query.

02:26.740 --> 02:30.980
And here in SQL, if you are using subquery with the comparison operators, we have a rule.

02:30.980 --> 02:33.980
The subquery must be a scalar subquery.

02:33.980 --> 02:35.460
So only one single value.

02:35.460 --> 02:41.380
So that's all about how to use the subquery in the Where clause using the comparison operators.

02:41.420 --> 02:41.700
All right.

02:41.700 --> 02:43.380
So now we have again the same task.

02:43.380 --> 02:50.020
And it says find the products that have a price higher than the average price of oil products.

02:50.020 --> 02:54.140
We have solved this task already using the subquery inside the From clause.

02:54.140 --> 02:57.540
But now we're going to go and solve it again using the subquery.

02:57.540 --> 02:59.740
But this time inside the Where clause.

02:59.740 --> 03:01.540
So let's do it step by step.

03:01.540 --> 03:03.980
Let's go and get the information that we need.

03:03.980 --> 03:11.260
So we need the product ID we need the price from the table sales products.

03:11.260 --> 03:12.660
So let's go and execute it.

03:12.660 --> 03:14.980
So now we got the list of all products.

03:14.980 --> 03:20.020
But we have to go and filter those informations using the column price.

03:20.020 --> 03:21.060
So that's in the results.

03:21.060 --> 03:24.040
We got all the products, but we don't need all the products.

03:24.040 --> 03:28.040
We need only the products where the price is higher than the average.

03:28.200 --> 03:32.760
That means we have to go and filter the table based on the values of the price.

03:32.760 --> 03:36.600
So now in order to do that, what we're going to do, we're going to use the Where clause.

03:36.600 --> 03:39.720
And we have to go and filter the data based on the price.

03:39.720 --> 03:43.840
And since we need higher than we're going to go and use the operator higher than.

03:43.840 --> 03:47.200
And now next we need the value average price.

03:47.200 --> 03:48.080
So how are we going to do it.

03:48.120 --> 03:52.320
We don't have the average price like out of the box in the table products.

03:52.320 --> 03:54.120
We have to go and calculate it.

03:54.120 --> 04:00.840
That's why we're going to go and write another query where we're going to go and find the average price

04:01.800 --> 04:06.280
from the table sales products like this.

04:06.280 --> 04:09.400
So now let's go and highlight it and then execute it.

04:09.400 --> 04:12.760
And with that we got now the average price of all products.

04:12.760 --> 04:16.200
And as you can see in the output we have only one single value.

04:16.200 --> 04:18.560
So this is a scalar query.

04:18.560 --> 04:24.560
So now what we need we need this value in order to be used in order to filter the first query.

04:24.560 --> 04:29.160
So that's why the first query is the main query bigger.

04:29.400 --> 04:35.830
The second one is the subquery that's going to support the main query in order to filter the data.

04:35.830 --> 04:40.910
So now what we're going to do we're going to take the subquery and use it in the Where clause.

04:40.950 --> 04:43.470
And now of course we have to tell SQL this is a subquery.

04:43.470 --> 04:46.550
That's why we have to put it inside two parentheses.

04:46.550 --> 04:52.150
So with that we have the subquery inside the Where clause in order to filter the main query.

04:52.150 --> 04:54.190
So let's go and execute it.

04:54.190 --> 04:59.790
And now as you can see in the output we have now only two products where the price is higher than the

04:59.830 --> 05:00.870
average price.

05:00.870 --> 05:02.270
So with that we have solved the task.

05:02.270 --> 05:07.350
But this time using the subquery in the Where clause in order to filter the main query.

05:07.390 --> 05:12.950
And of course, in order to see this value in our selects, since it is scalar subquery, we can as

05:12.950 --> 05:17.230
well go over here and put it in our select just in order to see the value.

05:17.270 --> 05:19.150
So average price.

05:19.310 --> 05:20.790
So let's go and execute it.

05:20.790 --> 05:24.150
And with that we can see as well in our results the average price.

05:24.150 --> 05:29.070
So this is how we use the subquery in the Where clause using the comparison operator okay.

05:29.110 --> 05:32.230
So let's see quickly how SQL execute our query step by step.

05:32.230 --> 05:35.630
So as usual first SQL is going to go and identify the subquery.

05:35.750 --> 05:38.620
It's going to be our select average price and so on.

05:38.660 --> 05:42.140
And now the next step is going to go and execute our subquery.

05:42.140 --> 05:43.660
So it is based on the products.

05:43.660 --> 05:49.140
And since we are doing aggregations without groupby at the output we will get only one value.

05:49.380 --> 05:50.980
So the average is going to be 20.

05:51.020 --> 05:53.620
This value is stored intermediately in the memory.

05:53.620 --> 05:55.420
So we will not see it in the output.

05:55.540 --> 05:59.100
SQL can go and pass this value to the main query.

05:59.100 --> 06:01.020
So the main query is going to look like this.

06:01.060 --> 06:03.900
We are selecting few columns from the table.

06:03.900 --> 06:10.220
And we are filtering the database on the price that is higher than the value 20 that we got it from

06:10.220 --> 06:11.020
the subquery.

06:11.020 --> 06:15.580
So now once SQL have everything for the main query SQL can go and execute it.

06:15.580 --> 06:21.340
So SQL going to go to the products and only select the products where the price is higher than 20.

06:21.340 --> 06:22.900
So it's only those two rows.

06:22.940 --> 06:27.300
And in the outputs we will get the final results the two products as well.

06:27.340 --> 06:29.060
So product ID and product price.

06:29.060 --> 06:29.660
So that's it.

06:29.660 --> 06:30.420
It's very simple.

06:30.420 --> 06:32.260
This is how SQL executed our query.

06:32.260 --> 06:37.860
So as usual first starting with the subquery, passing the value to the main query and other ends,

06:37.860 --> 06:41.740
the main query can be executed with the information from the subquery.

06:41.740 --> 06:43.780
And we will get at the end the final results.

06:43.780 --> 06:44.780
So that's it.
